# width: 120
# `ui.dock.mode = .always` (src/app/launcher_dock.zig): the launcher
# dock — macOS's Dock, not the bottom panel and not the dock widgets —
# reserves the OUTERMOST row of the editor area and lists the enabled
# integrations, then the terminals, then whatever `ui.dock.pins` names.
#
# At 120x40 that row is 37 — the EDITOR AREA's last row
# (`ui.dock.placement = .inner`, the default), so the statusline stays
# on 38 and the `:` line on 39, exactly where they sit with no dock at
# all. The click below is the proof: if the strip were anywhere else it
# would land on the editor instead.
# `launcher_dock_always_outer.test` is the other placement, where the
# strip takes the SCREEN's last row and those two move up one.
#
# The run is centred (`ui.dock.align`, `.center` out of the box) and the
# `+` ends it (dock-polish). // changed (railmove): the four first-party
# surfaces are on it, hidden chips and all, so at 120 the row is
# ` Browser  Claude Code  Codex  HTTP  New terminal  + New ` from
# column 25: Browser at 25..35, Claude Code at 36..50, Codex at 51..59,
# HTTP at 60..67, New terminal at 68..83, the `+` at 84..90. The click
# below lands after `:dock start` slides the run to column 1 — New
# terminal at 44..59, column 50 its word — and no click may land on
# 12..35, which would start a real `claude` / `codex`.
write a.txt "alpha\n"
open a.txt
expect screen lacks "New terminal"
ex dock always
expect screen contains "dock: always"
# The four first-party surfaces lead the strip, Browser first; the
# terminals follow them, and the `+` — the tab bar's own — ends the run.
expect screen contains "󰐕 New "
expect screen contains "Browser"
expect screen contains "New terminal"
# The pin chip lives at the strip's end.
expect screen contains "󰐃"
# // changed (dock-polish): the colours are the tab bar's. Anchored at
# the start (Browser's glyph at 2, New terminal's at 45 — Browser at
# 1..11, Claude Code 12..26, Codex 27..35, HTTP 36..43, New terminal
# 44..59), Browser's
# glyph is its chip's blue and the terminal's ghost is the split
# cluster's chip colour — the terminal's own bright white, palette
# index 15 — not the green the dock used to give it.
ex dock start
expect color 2 37 fg #61afef
expect color 45 37 fg index 15
expect color 45 37 fg not #98c379
# A click on New terminal opens a shell…
click 50 37
wait 900
expect pane Terminal
# …and the shell joins the strip as a running item at 60.. — the same
# ghost at 61, the same colour — told from the idle ones by brightness,
# with no dot on the row. (`●` is elsewhere on every screen — the tree's
# dirty dot, the TABS chip — so it is the SMALL dot that is checked.)
expect color 61 37 fg index 15
expect screen lacks "•"
# `:dock mark dot` brings the small dot back, in the ITEM's colour —
# the ghost's white, never green. Under `.icon_label` it takes the cell
# after the glyph (62; the icon and word forms put it in the padding
# cell before); `:dock mark bright` takes it away again.
ex dock mark dot
expect screen contains "dock: running mark is a small dot"
expect screen contains "•"
expect color 61 37 fg index 15
expect color 62 37 fg index 15
expect color 62 37 fg not #98c379
ex dock mark bright
expect screen contains "dock: running mark is the bright icon"
expect screen lacks "•"
